{"name":"Explorer of the Galaxies","hex":"#3a1f76","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"explorer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who explores something","A person who by means of travel (notably an expedition) searches out new information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/explorer"},{"term":"galaxies","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of galaxy"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/galaxies"}],"entries":[{"title":"Exploration","blocks":[{"text":"Exploration is the process of exploring, an activity which has some expectation of discovery. Organised exploration is largely a human activity, but exploratory activity is common to most organisms capable of directed locomotion and the ability to learn, and has been described in, amongst others, social insects foraging behaviour, where feedback from returning individuals affects the activity of other members of the group."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploration"},{"title":"Galaxy","blocks":[{"text":"A galaxy is a physical system of stars, planetary systems, stellar remnants, substellar objects, interstellar gas, dust, and dark matter bound together by gravity. The word is derived from the Greek galaxias (γαλαξίας), meaning 'milky', a reference to the Milky Way galaxy that contains the Solar System. Galaxies, averaging an estimated 100 million stars, range in size from dwarfs with less than a thousand stars to the largest galaxies known—supergiants with one hundred trillion stars, each orbiting its galaxy's center of mass. Most of the mass in a typical galaxy is in the form of dark matter, with only a few percent of that mass visible in the form of stars and nebulae. Supermassive black holes are a common feature at the centers of galaxies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/NGC_4414_%28NASA-med%29.jpg/330px-NGC_4414_%28NASA-med%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=